{"success": true, "message": "Test details sent successfully", "data": {"test": {"_id": "69de21f07e39d99b57bc5b03", "analysisVideoCdn": "yt", "exam": ["605dfcb2f226ac25a8bd5fef"], "ctype": null, "position": 0, "order": 12, "enableCalculator": false, "isContinuousQuestion": false, "reattemptAvailable": false, "singleSectionMultipleSubjects": false, "timeRestricted": false, "title": "Ray Optics - Test 7", "description": "Previous Year Questions as Chapter Test", "syllabus": "This test contains questions from all topics of this chapter included in the JEE Main syllabus. It includes a mix of questions from PYQs of JEE Main 2019 - 2025.", "totalTime": 40, "totalQuestions": 20, "totalMarks": 80, "bonusQuestions": null, "bonusMarks": null, "startDate": "2026-04-15T03:30:00.000Z", "endDate": "2027-04-30T00:00:00.000Z", "type": "free", "class": "605df635a37b7025c438d685", "instruction": "608289ffb160b022e9442c83", "subjects": [{"subjectId": "607018ee404ae53194e73d90", "totalMarks": 80, "bonusMarks": 0, "totalQuestions": 20, "bonusQuestions": 0, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f60d"}], "sections": [{"sectionId": {"_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f60a", "title": "Physics Numerical", "sectionType": "normal", "subject": "607018ee404ae53194e73d90", "questions": [{"_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f4f4", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4755"], "concepts": ["60e2fb1f66f9ce3f243b4b30"], "question": {"text": "A parallel beam of light is allowed to fall on a transparent spherical globe of diameter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>30</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math> and refractive index <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn></math>. The distance from the centre of the globe at which beam of light can converge is _____&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>mm</mi></math>."}, "options": [{"id": "d896eb", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f4f5"}, {"id": "dc1e86", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f4f6"}, {"id": "b91fa3", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f4f7"}, {"id": "f2cd46", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f4f8"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f4f9", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fbdc66f9ce3f243b5677"], "concepts": ["60e56739499d1e49588f8b4f"], "question": {"text": "<p>A pole is vertically submerged in swimming pool, such that it gives a length of shadow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>15</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math> within water when sunlight is incident at an angle of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>30</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math> with the surface of water. If swimming pool is filled to a height of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>,</mo></math> then the height of the pole above the water surface in centimeters is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>4</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>_</mi><mi>_</mi><mi>_</mi><mi>_</mi><mi>_</mi><mi>_</mi><mi>_</mi><mi>_</mi><mo>.</mo></math></p>"}, "options": [{"id": "c155d8", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f4fa"}, {"id": "e41ba0", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f4fb"}, {"id": "118294", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f4fc"}, {"id": "8e22eb", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f4fd"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f4fe", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60da9b4f62f042332c538326"], "concepts": ["6162fb4d6ed13040ec84d50c"], "question": {"text": "Cross-section view of a prism is the equilateral triangle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi></math> shown in the figure. The minimum deviation is observed using this prism when the angle of incidence is equal to the prism angle. The time taken by light to travel from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi></math> (midpoint of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi></math>) to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi></math> is&nbsp;___________&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mo>.</mo></math>&nbsp;(Given, speed of light in vacuum <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>8</mn></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>cos</mi><mn>30</mn><mo>&#176;</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>)<br /><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2Fe428e013-9369-4ca7-afb8-c3705bf70ea2-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 223px; height: 212px;\" />"}, "options": [{"id": "3cd360", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f4ff"}, {"id": "f3f6f1", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f500"}, {"id": "84069c", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f501"}, {"id": "a2e881", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f502"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f503", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b474f"], "concepts": ["60e2fb1f66f9ce3f243b4b22"], "question": {"text": "A compound microscope consists of an objective lens of focal length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math> and an eye piece of focal length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math> with a separation of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi><mo>.</mo></math> The distance between an object and the objective lens, at which the strain on the eye is minimum is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mn>40</mn></mfrac><mi>cm</mi><mo>.</mo></math> The value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi></math> is......."}, "options": [{"id": "69dffe", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f504"}, {"id": "14b78f", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f505"}, {"id": "0d8f6b", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f506"}, {"id": "da13d8", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f507"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f508", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fb5766f9ce3f243b4f39"], "concepts": ["60e5671a499d1e49588f87c2"], "question": {"text": "In an experiment for estimating the value of focal length of converging mirror, image of an object placed at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>40</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math> from the pole of the mirror is formed at distance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>120</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math> from the pole of the mirror. These distances are measured with a modified scale in which there are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>20</mn></math> small divisions in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math>. The value of error in measurement of focal length of the mirror is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>K</mi></mfrac><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math>. The value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>K</mi></math> is ______."}, "options": [{"id": "269256", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f509"}, {"id": "af1bdc", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f50a"}, {"id": "d4602b", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f50b"}, {"id": "4ec2be", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f50c"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f50d", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fbdc66f9ce3f243b5677"], "concepts": ["60e567dc499d1e49588f9cb3"], "question": {"text": "<p>Two immiscible liquids of refractive indices <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>8</mn><mn>5</mn></mfrac></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math> respectively are put in a beaker as shown in the figure. The height of each column is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>6</mn><mo> </mo><mi>cm</mi></math>. A coin is placed at the bottom of the beaker. For near normal vision, the apparent depth of the coin is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi>α</mi><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo> </mo><mi>cm</mi></math>. The value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>α</mi></math> is _______.</p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2F074022ce-71e2-4279-b8fd-0cfb99e6845e.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 300px; height: 247px;\"/>"}, "options": [{"id": "b13d96", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f50e"}, {"id": "76a995", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f50f"}, {"id": "433a85", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f510"}, {"id": "cdead2", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f511"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f512", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4755"], "concepts": ["617aa9daef3fe65ad8e829f9"], "question": {"text": "<p>The graph between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>u</mi></mfrac></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>v</mi></mfrac></math> for a thin convex lens in order to determine its focal length is plotted as shown in the figure. The refractive index of lens&nbsp;is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn></math>&nbsp;and its both the surfaces have same radius of curvatures <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi></math>. The value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi></math> will be _____ <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>cm</mi></math>.</p><p>(Where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo></math> object distance, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo></math> image distance)</p><p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2F4789e12f-5151-4569-8ed1-2f4edd8182e7-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 400px; height: 299px;\" /></p>"}, "options": [{"id": "9403d3", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f513"}, {"id": "2fe386", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f514"}, {"id": "a9fe5d", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f515"}, {"id": "fd9437", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f516"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f517", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60da9b4f62f042332c538326"], "concepts": ["60e2fbdc66f9ce3f243b565e"], "question": {"text": "<p>A deviation of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math> is produced in the yellow ray when prism of crown and flint glass are achromatically combined. Taking dispersive powers of crown and flint glass are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>02</mn></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>03</mn></math>&nbsp;respectively and refractive index for yellow light for these glasses are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>6</mn></math> respectively. The refracting angles for crown glass prism will be ________<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#176;</mo></math> (in degree)</p><p>(Round off to the Nearest Integer)</p>"}, "options": [{"id": "b97349", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f518"}, {"id": "a1a0be", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f519"}, {"id": "4be30f", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f51a"}, {"id": "7f56ad", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f51b"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f51c", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fb5766f9ce3f243b4f39"], "concepts": ["6162fb486ed13040ec84d13d"], "question": {"text": "A thin cylindrical rod of length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math> is placed horizontally on the principle axis of a concave mirror of focal length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>20</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math>. The rod is placed in a such a way that mid point of the rod is at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>40</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math> from the pole of mirror. The length of the image formed by the mirror will be&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math>.&nbsp;The value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi></math> is ______."}, "options": [{"id": "b581da", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f51d"}, {"id": "fee365", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f51e"}, {"id": "96fb9e", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f51f"}, {"id": "60d868", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f520"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f521", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b474f"], "concepts": ["60e2fb1f66f9ce3f243b4b22"], "question": {"text": "In a compound microscope, the magnified virtual image is formed at a distance of <math><mrow><mn>25</mn><mtext>&#160;cm</mtext></mrow></math> from the eye-piece. The focal length of its objective lens is <math><mrow><mn>1</mn><mtext>&#160;cm</mtext></mrow></math>. If the magnification is <math><mrow><mn>100</mn></mrow></math> and the tube length of the microscope is <math><mrow><mn>20</mn><mtext>&#160;cm</mtext></mrow></math>, then the focal length of the eye-piece lens (in <math><mrow><mtext>cm</mtext></mrow></math>) is ______"}, "options": [{"id": "e8d28c", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f522"}, {"id": "210e93", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f523"}, {"id": "b1e3e1", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f524"}, {"id": "d6d386", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f525"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f526", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4755"], "concepts": ["6162fb4c6ed13040ec84d402"], "question": {"text": "Light from a point source in air falls on a convex curved surface of radius <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>20</mn><mo> </mo><mi>cm</mi></math> and refractive index <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn></math>. If the source is located at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>100</mn><mo> </mo><mi>cm</mi></math> from the convex surface, the image will be formed at ______ <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>cm</mi></math> from the object."}, "options": [{"id": "006f29", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f527"}, {"id": "33f236", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f528"}, {"id": "8a094c", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f529"}, {"id": "74e296", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f52a"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f52b", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fbdc66f9ce3f243b5677"], "concepts": ["60e2fbdc66f9ce3f243b565d"], "question": {"text": "A small bulb is placed at the bottom of a tank containing water to a depth of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msqrt><mn>7</mn></msqrt><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math>. The refractive index of water is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>4</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></math>. The area of the surface of water through which light from the bulb can emerge out is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi><mi>&#960;</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math>. The value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi></math>&nbsp;is _____ ."}, "options": [{"id": "22e53c", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f52c"}, {"id": "02bab4", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f52d"}, {"id": "aa0897", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f52e"}, {"id": "3a615f", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f52f"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f530", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60da9b4f62f042332c538326"], "concepts": ["60e2fb9d66f9ce3f243b52b6"], "question": {"text": "<p>A thin prism of angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>6</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>&nbsp;and refractive index for yellow light <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><msub><mi>n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Y</mi></msub></mfenced><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn></math>&nbsp;is combined with another prism of angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Y</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>55</mn></math>. The combination produces no dispersion. The net average deviation&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi>&#948;</mi></mfenced></math>&nbsp;produced by the combination is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mfenced><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>x</mi></mfrac></mfenced><mo>&#176;</mo></msup></math>. The value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi></math> is _____ .</p><p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2Fcbfce54d-a710-4902-bbf3-af235dd83a3d-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 220px; height: 179px;\" /></p>"}, "options": [{"id": "c8d5c2", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f531"}, {"id": "28c0a7", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f532"}, {"id": "9bdd74", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f533"}, {"id": "89ef6d", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f534"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f535", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4755"], "concepts": ["60e567aa499d1e49588f95c2"], "question": {"text": "The same size images are formed by a convex lens when the object is placed at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>20</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math> or at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math> from the lens. The focal length of convex lens is"}, "options": [{"id": "e3174b", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f536"}, {"id": "283880", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f537"}, {"id": "dfe380", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f538"}, {"id": "62e7dd", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f539"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f53a", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fbdc66f9ce3f243b5677"], "concepts": ["60e56739499d1e49588f8b4f"], "question": {"text": "<p>An observer can see through a small hole on the side of a jar (radius <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>15</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math> ) at a point at height of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>15</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math>&nbsp;from the bottom (see figure). The hole is at a height of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>45</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math>. When the jar is filled with a liquid up to a height of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>30</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math> the same observer can see the edge at the bottom of the jar. If the refractive index of the liquid is<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mn>100</mn></mfrac><mo>,</mo></math> where&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></math> is an integer, the value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi></math> is</p><p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2F4fac4e03-34b4-425e-9745-186ed7040470-image.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 253px; height: 248px;\" /></p>"}, "options": [{"id": "edcdae", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f53b"}, {"id": "c65229", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f53c"}, {"id": "14a345", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f53d"}, {"id": "b53d1a", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f53e"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f53f", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4755"], "concepts": ["60e567dc499d1e49588f9cb4"], "question": {"text": "A bi convex lens of focal length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math> is cut in two identical parts along a plane perpendicular to the principal axis. The power of each lens after cut is _____ <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi></math>."}, "options": [{"id": "9b2392", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f540"}, {"id": "081bbb", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f541"}, {"id": "6dd7b8", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f542"}, {"id": "f2a92a", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f543"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f544", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fbdc66f9ce3f243b5677"], "concepts": ["60e2fbdc66f9ce3f243b565d"], "question": {"text": "<p>A fish rising vertically upward with a uniform velocity of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>8</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>&#8211;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo></math> observes that a bird is diving vertically downward towards the fish with the velocity of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>12</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>&#8211;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>.</mo></math> If the refractive index of water is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>4</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo>,</mo></math> then the&nbsp;actual velocity of the diving bird to pick the fish, will be<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>__________m</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>.</mo></math></p><p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2Fda935ce2-68bd-42d9-8752-80cb464f7da1.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 200px; height: 197px;\" /></p>"}, "options": [{"id": "7e3482", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f545"}, {"id": "62050b", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f546"}, {"id": "90de43", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f547"}, {"id": "dd648c", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f548"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f549", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4755"], "concepts": ["60e2fad966f9ce3f243b4707"], "question": {"text": "<p>A point object&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>O</mi></math> is placed in front of two thin symmetrical coaxial convex lenses <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> with focal length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>24</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math> and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>9</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math> respectively. The distance between two lenses is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math> and the object is placed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>6</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math> away from lens <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math> as shown in the figure. The distance between the object and the image formed by the system of two lenses is _____________&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>cm</mi></math></p><p><img src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fjee_main%2Fa6a97259-00c7-4ebe-9258-574febd02268.png?alt=media\" style=\"width: 300px; height: 174px;\" /></p>"}, "options": [{"id": "b22671", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f54a"}, {"id": "cb4ae5", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f54b"}, {"id": "3fdca4", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f54c"}, {"id": "e8175a", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f54d"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f54e", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fbdc66f9ce3f243b5677"], "concepts": ["60e56739499d1e49588f8b4f"], "question": {"text": "A light ray is incident on a glass slab of thickness $4 \\sqrt{3} \\mathrm{~cm}$ and refractive index $\\sqrt{2}$ . The angle of incidence is equal to the critical angle for the glass slab with air. The lateral displacement of ray after passing through glass slab is _____ $\\mathrm{cm}$. $\\left(\\right.$ Given $\\left.\\sin 15^{\\circ}=0.25\\right)$"}, "options": [{"id": "2ac2a4", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f54f"}, {"id": "ad7bfe", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f550"}, {"id": "3ea018", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f551"}, {"id": "7c0dbe", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f552"}], "questionLabels": []}, {"_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f553", "type": "numerical", "chapters": ["60da9b5062f042332c538358"], "topics": ["60e2fada66f9ce3f243b4755"], "concepts": ["60e2fb1f66f9ce3f243b4b30"], "question": {"text": "The distance between object and its two times magnified real image as produced by a convex lens is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>45</mn><mo> </mo><mi>cm</mi></math>. The focal length of the lens used is ________<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>cm</mi></math>."}, "options": [{"id": "18caed", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f554"}, {"id": "0557c7", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f555"}, {"id": "e7fd05", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f556"}, {"id": "42ac30", "text": "", "isCorrect": null, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f557"}], "questionLabels": []}], "markingScheme": {"_id": "608283c623cf4b3ad8f125e5", "title": "JEE Numerical", "correctAttempt": 4, "incorrectAttempt": -1, "createdAt": "2021-04-09T08:45:27.797Z", "updatedAt": "2021-07-05T05:39:27.056Z", "__v": 0, "updated_by": "6042252509c5bf34e8a2f178"}, "maximumAttemptLimit": 20, "linkedQuestions": [], "createdAt": "2026-04-14T14:30:03.459Z", "updatedAt": "2026-04-14T14:30:03.459Z", "__v": 0}, "position": 0, "_id": "69de4f6b2ee0e063d927f60c"}], "bonusSections": [], "packDetails": [], "paidFeatures": [], "tags